  "interpretation": [
    "Only two of twenty-eight reference-policy cases are feasible; they are uniform and tail-hot access for the same SUBRECT-NONE-1024-60 encoded-source-retained combination.",
    "No synthetic scenario is feasible at the 32 MiB / 8-frame point under both retention strategies, so the reference point has no fully feasible scenario.",
    "The feasible 1024x1024, 60-frame source-retained case uses eight logical checkpoint starts, seven retained 4 MiB canvases plus a 4 MiB encoded source, exactly 32 MiB retained state, and a 44 MiB modeled peak including working and output canvases.",
    "Full-canvas checkpoints dominate sparse 2048x2048 cases: a 16 MiB canvas multiplied by the checkpoint count overwhelms the small raw subrect payload. SPARSE-2048-256 requires 512 MiB at an eight-frame replay bound even when only the encoded source is retained.",
    "Raw-subrect retention is useful for exact composition and sequential playback, but retaining all raw frames plus full-canvas checkpoints is not a general bounded random-access policy under the current budgets.",
    "The next candidate must reduce checkpoint representation cost, for example persistent tiles, bounded dirty-region snapshots, or independently validated compressed checkpoints; merely increasing checkpoint spacing trades memory for replay without solving both bounds."
  ],
  "objective": "Determine whether a raw-subrect APNG compositor can bound random-access replay with retained full-canvas pre-frame checkpoints under the current 32 MiB Fovea animation-frame reference budget and wider 8-512 MiB sensitivity grid.",
